(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_electromagneticinduction=[{id:"neet-2026-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Induced emf and power in a loop",q:"A conducting loop of finite resistance lies on the x \u2212 y plane. There is a constant magnetic field in the z direction. The area of the loop varies with time t, as A = A\u2080(1 + sin t) in appropriate units. The figure that correctly indicates the qualitative behaviour of the power P dissipated in the loop as a function of time is:",o:["Graph (1): P starts at a non-zero value, falls in a concave-down arc to zero (cusp), then rises again","Graph (2): P is constant (horizontal line)","Graph (3): P rises smoothly and increasingly from zero","Graph (4): P shows two humps, zero at t = 0, a zero in between, and zero at the end"],c:0,s:`A = A\u2080(1 + sin t).
Flux \u03C6 = BA = BA\u2080(1 + sin t).
Emf \u03B5 = \u2212d\u03C6/dt = \u2212BA\u2080 cos t.
I_induced = \u03B5/R = \u2212(BA\u2080/R) cos t.
Power P = I\xB2R = B\xB2A\u2080\xB2cos\xB2t/R.
So P \u221D cos\xB2t: non-zero at t = 0, falls to zero, then rises again (graph 1).`},{id:"neet-2026-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Self inductance of solenoid",q:"Consider a long solenoid of length l and radius r. If n is the number of turns per unit length and \u03BC\u2080 is the permeability of free space, the inductance of the solenoid is :",o:["\u03BC\u2080n\xB2r\xB2l","(\u03BC\u2080/2\u03C0)n\xB2r\xB2l","2\u03BC\u2080\u03C0n\xB2r\xB2l","\u03BC\u2080\u03C0n\xB2r\xB2l"],c:3,s:`Self inductance of solenoid: L = N\u03C6/i.
\u03C6 = BA, so L = NBA/i.
B = \u03BC\u2080ni, so L = N\u03BC\u2080niA/i, where n = N/l.
L = N\u03BC\u2080nA.
L = (N/l)\u03BC\u2080nAl.
L = \u03BC\u2080n\xB2Al.
Since A = \u03C0r\xB2, L = \u03BC\u2080n\xB2\u03C0r\xB2l.`},{id:"neet-2026-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Inductors in series and parallel",q:`Two identical inductors are connected in two different configurations P and Q, where a time varying current I(t) is flowing, as shown in the figure. The induced emf between points a and b for configuration P is EP and that for configuration Q is EQ. The ratio EP/EQ is:
[Neglect the effect of mutual inductance.]`,o:["1/2","1","2","1/4"],c:2,s:`In figure (P) the two inductors are in series, so the inductance between a and b is that of one inductor L.
EP = Eab = \u2212L dI(t)/dt.
In figure (Q) the two inductors are in parallel, giving L/2.
EQ = Eab = \u2212(L/2) dI(t)/dt.
EP/EQ = 2/1.`}]})();
